e statistically sound play. 6-2, even suited, has very poor equity pre-flop (~40% against a random hand, much worse against strong holdings). I'd only lose 100 chips (the big blind I already posted).\n\n2. **Check (0)**: Since my opponent just called (action: 0), I'm in the big blind and can see the flop for free. This is actually viable because:\n   - No additional chips required\n   - Small chance to hit a flush (if 3+ spades come)\n   - Very small chance to hit two pair or trips\n   - Can fold on the flop if I miss\n\n3. **Raise (200+)**: Aggressive bluffing with a terrible hand. While position and aggression matter, 6-2s is too weak to justify risking more chips pre-flop, especially when I'm already down -50 cumulative.\n\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Extremely risky with such a weak hand. Would risk all 19,900 chips on a hand that loses to most holdings.\n\nGiven that:\n- My opponent just called (showing no strength)\n- I can see the flop for FREE (check costs nothing)\n- I'm only on hand 5/20, so there's plenty of time to recover\n- A suited hand has ~6.5% chance to flop a flush draw\n\nReason:\nI should **check** and see the flop for free. While 6-2s is terrible, seeing a free flop gives me a chance to potentially improve (flush draw, miracle two pair, etc.) with zero additional risk. If the flop misses me completely (which is likely), I can fold to any bet. This is pot odds management - getting a free look is always +EV compared to folding when no additional investment is required.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
